<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pastoral Care</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 590</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Regula Pastoralis, Liber Regulae Pastoralis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pastoral Care (Regula Pastoralis) is a treatise written by Pope <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-i">Gregory the Great</a> around 590 on the duties and conduct of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> and clergy. Widely read in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, it was translated into Old English in the circle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a>, with a preface in the king's name lamenting the decline of learning in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and announcing his programme of translations.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pastoral-care.jpg" alt="Pastoral Care" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gregory the Great, unknown writer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:01_01B_504_4TroyesMS504PastoralCare.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Gregory wrote the book shortly after his papal inauguration, in answer to a query from John, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Ravenna, to whom it is addressed. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> title, Liber Regulae Pastoralis or Book of Pastoral Rule, was the one he used when he sent a copy to his friend Leander of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, and he later revised the text somewhat. It sets out the personal, intellectual and moral standards he expected of parish priests.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Some thought these standards unrealistic for the conditions of the 6th century: a letter from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Cartagena, preserved in Gregory's correspondence, praises the book but doubts it is within ordinary capacities. Its influence was nonetheless vast. After reading it, the Byzantine emperor Maurice had it translated into Greek and sent to every bishop of the empire; of all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> authors of the patristic period, only Gregory had works translated into Greek in his own lifetime.</p><p>In the West it was recommended to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>'s bishops at a series of councils in 813, and Hincmar, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Rheims</a> from 845 to 882, records that a copy was placed in the hands of bishops before the altar at their consecration, with the Book of Canons. In the late 9th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a> translated it into Old English, noting in his preface that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustine-of-canterbury">Augustine of Canterbury</a> had brought it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and wished every bishop in his kingdom to have a copy. That translation, kept in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodleian-library">Bodleian Library</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, is the oldest surviving book written in English and was inscribed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s UK Memory of the World Register in May 2011.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Among the many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, perhaps the oldest is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troyes">Troyes</a>, Bibliothèque Municipale, MS 504, an early seventh-century copy probably made in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Written in uncial script with no spaces between words, about twenty-five long lines to the page, it holds the full revised text and is decorated only with penwork initials in red, green and yellow.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pastoral Care” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…books ‘most necessary for all men to know’ to be put into English, among them Gregory the Great’s <b>Pastoral Care</b>, with a preface in his own name; English versions of Boethius’s Consolation of Philosophy, of Orosius’s history and of Bede’s Ecclesiastical History were also made in this period.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wessex">Kingdom of Wessex · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Prose came later, inspired chiefly by Alfred the Great, who in the later 9th century advocated education in English alongside Latin and had many works translated, some, such as Pope Gregory I’s <b>Pastoral Care</b>, apparently by himself.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-english">Old English · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li></ul></section>
